  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 235 AM EDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nThe cold front has finally moved through the region and is over \nnorthern MS/AL/GA. Currently, a ridge is centered over the Great \nPlains and a trough is over the Northeast. The ridge will build into \nthe Ohio and Tennessee Valleys today as the trough exits the East \nCoast. Temperatures will be a bit cooler today (although still very \nwarm) with highs in the Tennessee Valley mainly in the upper 80s. \nShowers and storms will be isolated to scattered this afternoon \nmainly along and south of I-40 as an inverted surface trough forms \nover Kentucky and Tennessee. SPC has general thunder for that area \nand that seems appropriate.\n\nTuesday will be dry and a bit warmer with the ridge now centered \ncloser to the region over the Lower Mississippi Valley. Wednesday \nwill also be dry and warm but the ridge begins to flatten as a broad \ntrough moves into the Ohio Valley. Highs will be in the upper 80s \nand lower 90s which is a few degrees above normal.\n\nConfidence is increasing for scattered to widespread showers and \nstorms Thursday and Friday as a shortwave moves through the Ohio \nValley. A cold front will also move through the region around \nThursday night and may stall a bit to the south causing showers to \nlinger into Friday.\n\nThe weekend looks to start off with a weak trough possibly bringing \nshower and storm activity Saturday before a weak ridge on Sunday \nleads to drier weather.",
